Imagine you have a lemonade stand.

  You want to know: "If I spend 10 KWD on ads, how much lemonade will I sell?"

  So here's what the forecast does:

  1. It looks at the past. Last time you advertised lemonade, you spent 5 KWD and made 20 KWD back. So for every 1 KWD you spend, you get ~4 KWD back. That's your ROAS.
  2. It knows some days are better. Fridays people buy more, Mondays less. Summer is hot = more lemonade. Winter = less. So it adjusts up or down depending on when you want to run your ad.
  3. It knows spending more doesn't always mean more sales. If you normally spend 5 KWD/day and suddenly spend 50 KWD/day, you won't get 10x more sales. There are only so many people to show ads to. So it reduces
  the return the more you overspend compared to what you usually spend.
  4. If it doesn't know your product well (new product, not much history), it looks at similar products in the same category or with the same keywords and uses their numbers instead.
  5. It also adds organic sales — stuff you'd sell anyway even without ads, based on your sellout data.

  Then it does this calculation for every single day in your date range, adds it all up, and tells you: "You'll probably make X revenue, Y orders, and Z units."

  That's it. Past performance + seasonality + don't-overspend adjustment = prediction.
